1
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:03,692
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's a sutta here called the fāma.

2
00:00:10,578 --> 00:00:16,804
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, on that occasion, the Blessed One was instructing, exhorting, inspiring, gladdening the bhikkhus with a dhamma talk concerning nirvāṇa.

3
00:00:18,606 --> 00:00:25,412
[SPEAKER_02]: Those bhikkhus were listening to the dhamma with eager ears, attending to it as a matter of vital concern, applying their whole minds to it.

4
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:29,286
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it occurred to Mara, the evil one.

5
00:00:29,786 --> 00:00:33,166
[SPEAKER_02]: The ascetic Gautama is instructing the bhikkhus.

6
00:00:34,307 --> 00:00:37,687
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me approach the ascetic Gautama in order to confound him.

7
00:00:38,768 --> 00:00:51,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, Mara, the evil one, manifested himself in the form of a farmer, carrying a large plough on his shoulder, holding a large goat stick, his hair disheveled, wearing hempen garments, his feet smeared with mud.

8
00:00:52,510 --> 00:00:54,551
[SPEAKER_02]: He approached the Blessed One and said to him,

9
00:00:56,068 --> 00:00:58,290
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe you've seen oxen, ascetic.

10
00:00:59,831 --> 00:01:01,251
[SPEAKER_01]: What are oxen to you, either one?

11
00:01:02,993 --> 00:01:06,255
[SPEAKER_02]: So then he says, okay, the eye is mine, this is Mara.

12
00:01:06,395 --> 00:01:08,436
[SPEAKER_02]: The eye is mine, ascetic.

13
00:01:08,837 --> 00:01:09,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Forms are mine.

14
00:01:09,917 --> 00:01:13,360
[SPEAKER_02]: Eye contact and its base of consciousness are mine.

15
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:16,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Where can you go, ascetic, to escape from me?

16
00:01:18,317 --> 00:01:19,119
[SPEAKER_02]: The ear is mine.

17
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:19,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Ascetic.

18
00:01:20,222 --> 00:01:21,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Sounds are mine.

19
00:01:21,065 --> 00:01:22,087
[SPEAKER_02]: The nose is mine.

20
00:01:22,428 --> 00:01:23,331
[SPEAKER_02]: Tongue is mine.

21
00:01:23,391 --> 00:01:24,133
[SPEAKER_02]: Body is mine.

22
00:01:24,153 --> 00:01:25,076
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind is mine.

23
00:01:25,136 --> 00:01:25,597
[SPEAKER_02]: Ascetic.

24
00:01:25,657 --> 00:01:26,058
[SPEAKER_02]: Mental.

25
00:01:26,870 --> 00:01:27,991
[SPEAKER_02]: The phenomena are mine.

26
00:01:28,811 --> 00:01:32,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Mine contact and its base of consciousness are mine.

27
00:01:32,633 --> 00:01:34,815
[SPEAKER_02]: Where can you go, ascetic, to escape from me?

28
00:01:37,456 --> 00:01:40,418
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the Buddha says, the eye is yours, evil one.

29
00:01:40,838 --> 00:01:41,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Forms are yours.

30
00:01:42,379 --> 00:01:44,701
[SPEAKER_02]: Eye contact and its base of consciousness are yours.

31
00:01:45,281 --> 00:01:54,566
[SPEAKER_02]: But, evil one, where there is no eye, no forms, no eye contact and its base of consciousness, there is no place for you there, evil one.

32
00:01:55,907 --> 00:01:56,768
[SPEAKER_02]: The ear is yours.

33
00:02:01,410 --> 00:02:02,410
[SPEAKER_02]: The nose is yours.

34
00:02:04,071 --> 00:02:05,072
[SPEAKER_02]: The tongue is yours.

35
00:02:05,712 --> 00:02:06,693
[SPEAKER_02]: The body is yours.

36
00:02:09,714 --> 00:02:11,035
[SPEAKER_02]: Mind is yours, evil one.

37
00:02:12,056 --> 00:02:13,096
[SPEAKER_02]: Phenomena are yours.

38
00:02:13,657 --> 00:02:16,939
[SPEAKER_02]: Mind contact and its base of consciousness are yours.

39
00:02:17,259 --> 00:02:25,805
[SPEAKER_02]: But, evil one, where there is no mind, no phenomena, no mind contact and its base of consciousness, there is no place for you there, evil one.

40
00:02:26,605 --> 00:02:37,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Then there's a verse, Mara says, that of which they say it's mine,

41
00:02:38,928 --> 00:02:46,932
[SPEAKER_02]: And, and those who speak in terms of mine, if your mind exists among these, you won't escape me, ascetic.

42
00:02:48,913 --> 00:02:53,276
[SPEAKER_02]: And the Buddha says, that which they speak of is not mine.

43
00:02:55,057 --> 00:02:57,078
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not one of those who speak of mine.

44
00:02:57,718 --> 00:03:01,760
[SPEAKER_02]: You should know thus, oh evil one, even my path you will not see.

45
00:03:02,841 --> 00:03:04,862
[SPEAKER_02]: Then Mara, the evil one, disappeared right there.

46
00:03:07,907 --> 00:03:10,829
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it quite clearly illustrates where the problem is.

47
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:18,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem is not in the eye, in the sights, in the consciousness established upon it.

48
00:03:19,813 --> 00:03:27,837
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even in the pressure that eye exhibits, the sights exhibit on you, the contact, usually translated as contact, but it's really like a pressure.

49
00:03:30,459 --> 00:03:31,619
[SPEAKER_02]: None of those is a problem.

50
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:34,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Problem is regarding it as mind.

51
00:03:34,921 --> 00:03:35,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Problem is having

52
00:03:36,968 --> 00:03:39,369
[SPEAKER_01]: The assumption of ownership in regard to it.

53
00:03:40,769 --> 00:03:42,610
[SPEAKER_01]: And on many other occasions, that's what was said.

54
00:03:42,650 --> 00:03:46,832
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you take that which belongs to Mārā, Mārā does with you what he pleases.

55
00:03:47,532 --> 00:03:48,732
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it that belongs to Mārā?

56
00:03:48,832 --> 00:03:53,774
[SPEAKER_01]: I belongs to Mārā, sights belong to Mārā, I consciousness belong to Mārā, nose and so on, everything else.

57
00:03:56,235 --> 00:03:59,376
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you then escape Mārā?

58
00:03:59,576 --> 00:04:00,917
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you then escape suffering?

59
00:04:02,762 --> 00:04:06,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Without destroying eyes, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

60
00:04:08,244 --> 00:04:09,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Without avoiding contact.

61
00:04:12,245 --> 00:04:13,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Without giving in to pressure.

62
00:04:13,665 --> 00:04:15,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Without trying to deny pressure.

63
00:04:18,967 --> 00:04:20,227
[SPEAKER_02]: By not taking ownership of it.

64
00:04:22,308 --> 00:04:22,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

65
00:04:22,928 --> 00:04:24,468
[SPEAKER_01]: And how would that be done?

66
00:04:25,708 --> 00:04:27,189
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you not take the ownership?

67
00:04:28,749 --> 00:04:31,330
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, not taking ownership,

68
00:04:33,375 --> 00:04:34,155
[SPEAKER_01]: You can own that.

69
00:04:35,216 --> 00:04:37,036
[SPEAKER_01]: The attitude of nothing is mine.

70
00:04:37,877 --> 00:04:39,957
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

71
00:04:40,458 --> 00:04:44,299
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you see, like the Buddha's saying here, the I is yours.

72
00:04:45,679 --> 00:04:55,383
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you see that that which everything kind of depends on, isn't mine.

73
00:04:56,923 --> 00:05:00,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, just seeing that I's cannot be yours.

74
00:05:01,893 --> 00:05:03,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Sights cannot be yours.

75
00:05:03,154 --> 00:05:03,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

76
00:05:04,014 --> 00:05:10,758
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way to see that correctly, is to see that they cannot be yours, through your current mistake in ownership.

77
00:05:11,258 --> 00:05:12,779
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the important point.

78
00:05:13,419 --> 00:05:14,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Crucial point.

79
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:16,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the crucial point.

80
00:05:17,081 --> 00:05:23,425
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see... Because, see, you read that sutra and the Buddha said, I'm not the one who says mine, I don't use mine.

81
00:05:23,445 --> 00:05:25,206
[SPEAKER_01]: And people say, ah, so I must stop saying mine.

82
00:05:26,006 --> 00:05:28,888
[SPEAKER_01]: As if, just by not saying mine, you somehow transcend

83
00:05:29,778 --> 00:05:31,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although it's a mistake, it's real as such.

84
00:05:31,619 --> 00:05:34,659
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, you have to accept that ownership is there.

85
00:05:34,679 --> 00:05:36,060
[SPEAKER_01]: You regard your senses as yours.

86
00:05:54,635 --> 00:05:56,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Sites that please you, sites that displease you.

87
00:05:56,977 --> 00:06:01,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, you regard your body, the basis of your body, the basis of your experience, as belonging to you.

88
00:06:02,824 --> 00:06:11,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So, within that ownership, you then need to discern that these things are actually impossible to own.

89
00:06:11,753 --> 00:06:13,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in response to your ownership.

90
00:06:14,716 --> 00:06:30,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, ownership is a problem, so I will go around and keep telling myself that I can't own these things even if I want to, but the only reason you're doing that, or rather the reason you're doing that, is rooted in you trying to basically get rid of the ownership, and you own that, because you're acting out of the same ownership.

91
00:06:30,047 --> 00:06:30,828
[SPEAKER_01]: You're affirming it.

92
00:06:30,848 --> 00:06:31,748
[SPEAKER_01]: You're craving for it.

93
00:06:31,908 --> 00:06:32,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving against it.

94
00:06:32,829 --> 00:06:33,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is there.

95
00:06:35,511 --> 00:06:40,114
[SPEAKER_01]: So, accepting that there is ownership, and it's not a matter of choice, because as long as there is eyes, there is ownership.

96
00:06:40,154 --> 00:06:41,855
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as there are sights, there is ownership of it.

97
00:06:42,934 --> 00:06:56,212
[SPEAKER_01]: So, allowing that ownership to endure, but to see that it's dependent on something... Once you become skilled, so to speak, in not denying ownership, not acting blindly out of it,

98
00:06:56,985 --> 00:07:09,616
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh... seeing it for what it is, practicing restraint in regard to it, sure, but allowing it to endure, then you can get to see what's within that ownership, which is the basis, necessary basis for that ownership, which cannot be owned.

99
00:07:09,696 --> 00:07:11,357
[SPEAKER_02]: The basis for that ownership.

100
00:07:11,397 --> 00:07:13,299
[SPEAKER_02]: What makes that ownership possible in the first place?

101
00:07:13,915 --> 00:07:16,676
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is a necessary condition for the ownership of eyes and sights?

102
00:07:16,736 --> 00:07:18,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, things that please me.

103
00:07:18,237 --> 00:07:20,898
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go and look at something agreeable.

104
00:07:20,978 --> 00:07:23,520
[SPEAKER_01]: What is a necessary basis for that experience?

105
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:24,720
[SPEAKER_01]: The body.

106
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:26,021
[SPEAKER_01]: The body, exactly.

107
00:07:26,821 --> 00:07:30,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, more specifically, the eyes and things to be looked at.

108
00:07:31,303 --> 00:07:36,946
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, in half an hour, you want to go and, I don't know, watch a parade.

109
00:07:39,496 --> 00:07:44,878
[SPEAKER_01]: You see a movie, whatever it is, something you want visually pleasing for you.

110
00:07:45,318 --> 00:07:49,199
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have the prospect of engaging with sights.

111
00:07:51,340 --> 00:07:58,622
[SPEAKER_01]: And you set, you know, you accept it, you know, you get ready and you start going wherever that is happening.

112
00:07:58,642 --> 00:07:59,883
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

113
00:08:00,203 --> 00:08:01,783
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're already committed to that.

114
00:08:02,904 --> 00:08:07,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And from the point of, like, even before when you just decide to do it,

115
00:08:08,481 --> 00:08:09,601
[SPEAKER_01]: To engage with the sights.

116
00:08:11,922 --> 00:08:13,242
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a necessary basis.

117
00:08:13,463 --> 00:08:15,363
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a prospect of those sights, possibility.

118
00:08:15,383 --> 00:08:16,884
[SPEAKER_01]: They need to be there, to some extent.

119
00:08:17,684 --> 00:08:19,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Or at least, there is a likelihood of them being there.

120
00:08:20,725 --> 00:08:29,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your entire project of planning to engage, making effort to engage with with the sense object, is fully within necessary basis of those things.

121
00:08:29,947 --> 00:08:33,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Necessary container, necessary dependence upon those things.

122
00:08:34,069 --> 00:08:37,970
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you go there and, turns out, the movie is cancelled.

123
00:08:41,795 --> 00:08:46,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, this whole time, you were acting as if it were there.

124
00:08:47,177 --> 00:09:02,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that the necessary basis, doesn't matter how much you're in control, how many... like you can afford a ticket, you have... you can get there in time, you book your space, everything, all of these things you can control, are fully within the necessary base of things that fundamentally cannot be controlled.

125
00:09:03,522 --> 00:09:05,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Such as things get cancelled, things get destroyed.

126
00:09:06,783 --> 00:09:08,103
[SPEAKER_01]: You die on the way up there.

127
00:09:10,258 --> 00:09:23,828
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that might not be obvious to some, that the... it's not just sight... sights that have the eye as a necessary basis, but any possible... any possibility... Yeah.

128
00:09:24,389 --> 00:09:30,453
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on the level, that's where everything starts, basically, and stays, and maintains itself within the mind, enduring.

129
00:09:30,533 --> 00:09:30,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

130
00:09:31,174 --> 00:09:34,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you have the prospect of going to see the movie, there is no movie present here.

131
00:09:34,837 --> 00:09:35,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

132
00:09:36,386 --> 00:09:36,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a thought.

133
00:09:37,067 --> 00:09:38,328
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not seeing any of that.

134
00:09:38,768 --> 00:09:41,390
[SPEAKER_01]: But that thought stands for what your eyes are at the time.

135
00:09:41,931 --> 00:09:45,754
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, that's why all the senses, the five senses, meet in the mind.

136
00:09:45,994 --> 00:09:47,095
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where they're discerned.

137
00:09:47,395 --> 00:09:55,882
[SPEAKER_02]: And it may even be something that's not attended to, like, consciously attended to.

138
00:09:55,902 --> 00:09:58,525
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, like, people make decisions.

139
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:10,396
[SPEAKER_02]: And you don't need to sort of analyze it.

140
00:10:10,516 --> 00:10:17,463
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you just... Whatever you're about to do, you see it as an arisen prospect that endures.

141
00:10:18,223 --> 00:10:21,546
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can then exercise either restraint or giving into it.

142
00:10:22,607 --> 00:10:24,810
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's wholesome, you can do it.

143
00:10:25,650 --> 00:10:31,197
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's unwholesome, if it's rooted in desire, if it's rooted in distraction, you restrain yourself.

144
00:10:31,358 --> 00:10:31,918
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't do it.

145
00:10:32,299 --> 00:10:35,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, prospects will be in your mind for doing this or doing that.

146
00:10:35,223 --> 00:10:36,344
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot not do anything.

147
00:10:36,544 --> 00:10:38,187
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, I'm not doing anything that you're doing.

148
00:10:40,177 --> 00:10:56,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you have sort of eradicated engagement, careless engagement with prospects that are rooted in desire, then the ones that you can do, you then get to understand how, like, basically the threshold where your commitment sort of overreaches, so to speak.

149
00:10:56,286 --> 00:10:57,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I need to do this.

150
00:10:57,206 --> 00:10:57,726
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

151
00:10:59,067 --> 00:11:01,849
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't necessarily need to be emotionally invested in doing it.

152
00:11:03,710 --> 00:11:07,872
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if things change before you've completed it, before you've done it the way you wanted it, or

153
00:11:09,435 --> 00:11:12,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Whichever way it goes, you won't be emotionally affected.

154
00:11:13,636 --> 00:11:22,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you never lost the perspective that doesn't matter what you do, it's fundamentally rooted, contained within the basis, within the container that cannot be owned.

155
00:11:23,878 --> 00:11:27,339
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to arrive at true non-ownership of things is through ownership.

156
00:11:28,459 --> 00:11:33,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Through learning how to accept the ownership and allow it to endure and not blindly act towards it.

157
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:37,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Or trying to get rid of it, if it displeases you.

158
00:11:37,863 --> 00:11:38,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's another thing.

159
00:11:39,264 --> 00:11:43,888
[SPEAKER_01]: When you have a disagreeable experience affects you, that's why you're affected.

160
00:11:44,148 --> 00:11:45,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's yours.

161
00:11:47,832 --> 00:11:51,075
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to get rid of the owning unpleasant things.

162
00:11:52,228 --> 00:11:53,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I own this.

163
00:11:53,129 --> 00:11:53,749
[SPEAKER_01]: This is mine.

164
00:11:54,149 --> 00:11:56,130
[SPEAKER_01]: But look, it's turning really bad.

165
00:11:56,430 --> 00:11:57,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'll get rid of it.

166
00:11:57,231 --> 00:11:58,371
[SPEAKER_01]: I will stop owning it.

167
00:11:59,032 --> 00:12:00,312
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it would have meant in brief.

168
00:12:00,392 --> 00:12:06,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is being united with what we don't want and separated from things we want.

169
00:12:06,775 --> 00:12:10,958
[SPEAKER_01]: So, owning things you don't want and not owning things you want.

170
00:12:10,978 --> 00:12:12,398
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of every suffering.

171
00:12:13,919 --> 00:12:15,440
[SPEAKER_01]: What you want and what you don't want.

172
00:12:22,028 --> 00:12:27,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, the sense restraint needs to come first, because ownership is there, which means you're already acting out of it.

173
00:12:27,812 --> 00:12:34,416
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to stop acting out of it, in a sense, that would perpetuate that desire and anything unwholesome that's rooted in it.

174
00:12:35,196 --> 00:12:44,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It still won't free you from the ownership, but it will certainly set up things to be understood, as in understanding ownership in that sense that you're not emotionally invested in it.

175
00:12:46,223 --> 00:12:49,985
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the true ownership, true lack of ownership.

176
00:12:50,843 --> 00:13:00,531
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like throwing away things you own, destroying them, because then, if that were the case, you would say you just have to pull your eyes out, and you're free from ownership of sights.

177
00:13:00,551 --> 00:13:06,435
[SPEAKER_01]: The true ownership is not being pressured by those things, not owning the contact of them, not being contacted by them.

178
00:13:07,696 --> 00:13:16,103
[SPEAKER_02]: Mara says, that of which they say it's mine, and those who speak in terms of mine, if your mind exists among these, you would escape me.

179
00:13:16,123 --> 00:13:16,503
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

180
00:13:24,178 --> 00:13:41,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there is absolutely no feeling pressuring you, manifesting, arising, whatever, making you act on account of what's there in your experience, whether wanted or not wanted, you're not owning it.

181
00:13:44,635 --> 00:13:48,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's always... also you can use that as a criteria of, oh, well, am I attached to this?

182
00:13:50,873 --> 00:14:00,362
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were to change, if it were to become otherwise, if it were to be taken away, would I experience this agreeable feeling that would then overwhelm my mind?

183
00:14:00,422 --> 00:14:02,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I basically suffer on account of it?

184
00:14:03,945 --> 00:14:05,086
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not sure, means yes.

185
00:14:06,427 --> 00:14:09,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if doubt, means yes, you will suffer on account of it.

186
00:14:10,244 --> 00:14:12,645
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to be absolutely clear that... and that's what I mean.

187
00:14:12,665 --> 00:14:18,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can... you don't need to go and lose these things or you don't need to go and watch a movie to know that you're doing it out of desire.

188
00:14:18,646 --> 00:14:22,227
[SPEAKER_01]: You already know it when the prospect has arisen on the level of possibility.

189
00:14:23,627 --> 00:14:26,047
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to go to see that the movie is cancelled or not.

190
00:14:26,067 --> 00:14:27,648
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is you're acting out of the desire.

191
00:14:28,248 --> 00:14:33,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you don't get to see the movie, you still acted out of the desire on the level of your thought.

192
00:14:35,637 --> 00:14:41,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the thing got cancelled and you didn't actually engage to the extent you wanted to engage, you still engage with the desire.

193
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:44,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Because desire is not in those things, it's on the level of your mind.

194
00:14:45,402 --> 00:14:51,105
[SPEAKER_01]: So, same, then, you can imagine things that are dear to you or things that are threatening to you.

195
00:14:51,905 --> 00:14:53,306
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to go and engage with them.

196
00:14:53,386 --> 00:14:57,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Just think about it and you're already either affected or not.

197
00:14:57,648 --> 00:15:01,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Either craving to be free from it, either craving against it or craving for more of it.

198
00:15:09,684 --> 00:15:26,077
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like the sutta where they're in Jeta's wood in Samādhi and the Buddha says, would you be upset if someone came along and took away all those branches and twigs and leaves and things and said no?

199
00:15:26,117 --> 00:15:28,679
[SPEAKER_02]: Because why?

200
00:15:28,799 --> 00:15:30,300
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they're not ours.

201
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:32,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because you don't care about them.

202
00:15:32,742 --> 00:15:32,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

203
00:15:33,182 --> 00:15:36,545
[SPEAKER_01]: You absolutely have no emotional interest in random twigs.

204
00:15:36,785 --> 00:15:36,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

205
00:15:37,265 --> 00:15:38,987
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, well, that's how you should regard your body.

206
00:15:39,007 --> 00:15:39,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

207
00:15:40,248 --> 00:15:43,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Your eyes and everything else.

208
00:15:44,373 --> 00:15:55,166
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, it's certainly in the... in the beginning of that practice, the attitude will never... like, you know, somebody comes and reads the sutra, then immediately you think, okay, so ownership is bad.

209
00:15:55,526 --> 00:15:57,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, ownership is the root of suffering.

210
00:15:58,163 --> 00:16:05,868
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, whatever you do, you will... well, in the beginning, you won't be able to see that whatever you do will be on account of trying to get rid of the ownership.

211
00:16:06,068 --> 00:16:09,611
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way to get rid of the ownership is to accept it and understand it.

212
00:16:09,791 --> 00:16:12,052
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't understand it if you keep trying to get rid of it.

213
00:16:13,533 --> 00:16:14,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's not self, it's not self.

214
00:16:14,794 --> 00:16:16,175
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an atta, it's an atta.

215
00:16:16,235 --> 00:16:17,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, oh, look, look, look, that's attachment.

216
00:16:17,856 --> 00:16:18,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Just throw things away.

217
00:16:18,697 --> 00:16:19,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Just get rid of stuff.

218
00:16:19,477 --> 00:16:20,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Get rid of stuff.

219
00:16:20,677 --> 00:16:28,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you are not understanding ownership, because whenever you face it, you're just turning away, trying to deny it, get rid of it, get rid of it.

220
00:16:29,688 --> 00:16:37,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Isn't it like trying to treat a matter as a denial, so you can...

221
00:16:41,148 --> 00:16:42,668
[SPEAKER_01]: And provide an explanation for it.

222
00:16:42,728 --> 00:16:46,669
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not... they're not... they don't belong to you anyway, so that's... that's okay.

223
00:16:46,989 --> 00:16:47,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

224
00:16:47,429 --> 00:16:57,451
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole, you know, sexual intercourse is okay, because I'm not a... it's not me, it's just the body, it's animal instincts, that I'm an animal, a human animal.

225
00:16:58,191 --> 00:17:02,052
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's even more dishonest, basically.

226
00:17:02,132 --> 00:17:03,252
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, I know, I know.

227
00:17:03,372 --> 00:17:07,313
[SPEAKER_01]: But what I'm saying, like, you... you... you must have abandoned that attitude through your behavior.

228
00:17:08,193 --> 00:17:12,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Even before you can become aware of the ownership on its own terms.

229
00:17:12,275 --> 00:17:13,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the point.

230
00:17:13,075 --> 00:17:20,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, people can read and declare themselves Buddhist and then just say that nothing is real anyway, nothing belongs to me, I can do whatever I want.

231
00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:22,520
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

232
00:17:22,680 --> 00:17:25,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Only if you're an arahant, the statement, nothing belongs to me, is true.

233
00:17:26,602 --> 00:17:27,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, are you an arahant?

234
00:17:27,742 --> 00:17:27,942
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

235
00:17:28,202 --> 00:17:31,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you saying everything is an atta, is false.

236
00:17:31,084 --> 00:17:32,904
[SPEAKER_01]: From your experience, it's a lie.

237
00:17:34,809 --> 00:17:36,670
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, or wishful thinking.

238
00:17:36,690 --> 00:17:42,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Wishful thinking, sure, sure.

239
00:17:42,854 --> 00:17:48,137
[SPEAKER_02]: Based on the assumption that if I keep telling myself this, I'll be fine.

240
00:17:48,357 --> 00:17:49,618
[SPEAKER_01]: That, for me, it's very dishonest.

241
00:17:49,678 --> 00:17:51,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're lying to yourself by doing that.

242
00:17:52,140 --> 00:17:54,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Same way, oh, I can do these things without attachment.

243
00:17:55,522 --> 00:17:56,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, don't do them.

244
00:17:56,743 --> 00:17:57,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't do them at all.

245
00:17:58,224 --> 00:18:00,105
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no attachment, there is no pressure.

246
00:18:00,766 --> 00:18:02,767
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another... that's another sutra where the Buddha said...

247
00:18:03,348 --> 00:18:13,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes I ask monks to... to abandon some little trifle thing, something not so important, but it's still kind of an obstacle.

248
00:18:13,515 --> 00:18:16,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So the Buddha would ask them to... to get... stop doing that.

249
00:18:16,977 --> 00:18:19,899
[SPEAKER_01]: And then some monk said, well, look, this is nothing.

250
00:18:20,139 --> 00:18:21,480
[SPEAKER_01]: I can stop this whenever I want.

251
00:18:21,781 --> 00:18:23,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, he doesn't stop it.

252
00:18:24,813 --> 00:18:27,254
[SPEAKER_01]: And he gets fettered by it, because he didn't stop it.

253
00:18:27,695 --> 00:18:31,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Stopping it, doesn't matter what you think about it, stopping it will free you.

254
00:18:32,357 --> 00:18:33,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Not stopping it won't free you.

255
00:18:34,219 --> 00:18:40,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you think you can stop it anytime, and it's true, you can stop it anytime, and because of that thought, you don't stop it at any time.

256
00:18:40,942 --> 00:18:46,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, another monk says, oh, this is trifle, this is little, this is insignificant.

257
00:18:46,306 --> 00:18:48,007
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he makes the effort and he stops it.

258
00:18:48,947 --> 00:18:49,648
[SPEAKER_01]: He's free from it.

259
00:18:50,268 --> 00:18:53,310
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the simile, he said that the first monk, the one who doesn't stop it, is like a qu

260
00:18:55,308 --> 00:19:03,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Who is sort of tethered by the rotten ropes and tiny little strings, but the quail is weak, and that's more than enough to keep it high.

261
00:19:04,274 --> 00:19:08,516
[SPEAKER_01]: And the other one is like an elephant that gets tied by leather straps, but he still breaks them.

262
00:19:09,737 --> 00:19:12,199
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's often when people say, I can stop this whenever I want.

263
00:19:12,819 --> 00:19:15,541
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you can't want is to stop it whenever you want.

264
00:19:16,061 --> 00:19:18,363
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't want to want to stop it.

265
00:19:18,563 --> 00:19:23,526
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to want to stop it, you could stop it whenever you want, but you can't want to stop it.

266
00:19:24,280 --> 00:19:25,901
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you end up not stopping it.

267
00:19:27,662 --> 00:19:32,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, like, when people say, oh, you can have sexual intercourse without being attached, that's ridiculous.

268
00:19:32,285 --> 00:19:48,255
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, not just as Buddha said millions of times, that's impossible in the suttas, but also, like, how can you even engage to any extent of, like, physical pursuit of sensuality without being pressurized, without accepting it, without acting out of it, without... and that's already...

269
00:19:49,238 --> 00:19:50,319
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm saying.

270
00:19:50,859 --> 00:20:01,786
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even before you go and get to see the movie or see that the movie is cancelled, you've already engaged with sensual aspect of it by deciding to do it.

271
00:20:17,516 --> 00:20:18,898
[SPEAKER_01]: That's on the level of the thought.

272
00:20:18,938 --> 00:20:20,499
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already central perception.

273
00:20:20,559 --> 00:20:26,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So, central perception doesn't matter if it's the actual movie or of the thought of the possibility of the movie.

274
00:20:26,645 --> 00:20:31,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception of the visual domain, which is both actual and possible, is there.

275
00:20:31,930 --> 00:20:32,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, it's an image.

276
00:20:33,352 --> 00:20:33,952
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an image, sure.

277
00:20:33,972 --> 00:20:36,014
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of which, you've been pressured.

278
00:20:36,254 --> 00:20:36,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

279
00:20:37,135 --> 00:20:42,378
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you already accepted the desire on the level of your thought, and that thought remains enduring.

280
00:20:42,418 --> 00:20:43,179
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

281
00:20:43,679 --> 00:20:51,104
[SPEAKER_01]: If, say, your desire of... the thought of your desire, you accept it, and then it disappears, you wouldn't know where to go, what to do.

282
00:20:51,164 --> 00:20:57,328
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't have context for your action of seeking such and such movie, or entertainment, or group of people, or fun.

283
00:20:57,788 --> 00:20:58,649
[SPEAKER_01]: That desire remains.

284
00:20:59,851 --> 00:21:04,533
[SPEAKER_01]: And that desire makes every further engagement within it to be within that desire.

285
00:21:05,113 --> 00:21:09,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why you can't necessarily judge things externally, whether this is always desire, this is not always desire.

286
00:21:10,075 --> 00:21:11,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Sexually, because it's always desire.

287
00:21:12,316 --> 00:21:13,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Killing, it's always desire.

288
00:21:14,818 --> 00:21:18,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that basically are clearly against the precepts are always rooted in desire.

289
00:21:19,320 --> 00:21:25,002
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are sometimes things that are root... can be rooted in desire internally, but not always.

290
00:21:25,042 --> 00:21:26,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes they're not necessarily rooted in desire.

291
00:21:27,583 --> 00:21:30,806
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to only judge things externally, it means there's always desire.

292
00:21:31,286 --> 00:21:33,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, he has these possessions, he's always attached to it.

293
00:21:34,068 --> 00:21:36,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, he's eating, he's always craving for his food.

294
00:21:38,892 --> 00:21:50,042
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can actually not accept desire on the level of the thought, and then even if you eat, none of those actions that follow would... well, now they would be within non-acceptance of desire.

295
00:21:52,780 --> 00:21:59,243
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you practice restraint, if you accept desire on the level of thought, and then restrain yourself physically or verbally, you're still within the desire.

296
00:22:00,143 --> 00:22:01,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where desire is rooted.

297
00:22:02,004 --> 00:22:08,307
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the level of accepting those possibilities, those images in your mind, like, I want that, I don't want that.

298
00:22:08,347 --> 00:22:13,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Your restraint is determined by your desire.

299
00:22:13,729 --> 00:22:14,450
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you mean, sorry?

300
00:22:15,070 --> 00:22:16,931
[SPEAKER_02]: You mean, so even if you're restraining yourself,

301
00:22:18,552 --> 00:22:18,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

302
00:22:18,932 --> 00:22:23,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like, restraint is necessary and good, but yeah, it will still be within the desire.

303
00:22:25,076 --> 00:22:32,340
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when the mind is uprooted from that desire, or rather, desire is uprooted from the mind, then, yeah.

304
00:22:33,020 --> 00:22:36,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even if you're like, look, look all the nice food he's eating.

305
00:22:36,422 --> 00:22:37,422
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean he's attached.

306
00:22:37,462 --> 00:22:38,643
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha said himself.

307
00:22:40,144 --> 00:22:47,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes he gets those, you know, queens and kings serving with their own hands the finest possible food, and there's absolutely no desire in his mind.

308
00:22:48,107 --> 00:22:51,630
[SPEAKER_01]: He would sometimes eat a bowl full to the brim, sometimes two bowls full to the brim.

309
00:22:52,550 --> 00:22:55,513
[SPEAKER_01]: If he's hungry, there is a practical need for such amount of food.

310
00:22:55,573 --> 00:22:58,515
[SPEAKER_01]: If he walked for 30 kilometers across Bihar or something.

311
00:23:00,396 --> 00:23:01,577
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is absolutely no attachment.

312
00:23:01,597 --> 00:23:08,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you judge the food, the attachment by the amount of food you eat, then you wouldn't respect the Buddha.

313
00:23:09,943 --> 00:23:11,044
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a whole sutta about it.

314
00:23:11,524 --> 00:23:15,367
[SPEAKER_01]: When that man was praising him, he said, oh, his disciples eat little and he eats little.

315
00:23:15,387 --> 00:23:17,409
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, no, I have disciples who don't eat for days.

316
00:23:18,401 --> 00:23:25,167
[SPEAKER_01]: And I would sit and the Queen would serve me herself, like to the brim, full bowl of whatever you can possibly imagine.

317
00:23:25,207 --> 00:23:27,188
[SPEAKER_01]: If they were to respect me because of that, they wouldn't.

318
00:23:28,810 --> 00:23:31,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same with the lodgings and the medicines and so on.

319
00:23:32,653 --> 00:23:38,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the root of the desire is in the root of ownership, which is on the level of your mind.

320
00:23:41,100 --> 00:23:46,645
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you were like, say, practicing or maintaining, recollecting the context of death,

321
00:23:48,037 --> 00:23:56,702
[SPEAKER_01]: When you have a thought of going to watch a movie, and in the back of your mind, you refuse to abandon the context that you might die within the next out-breath.

322
00:23:58,823 --> 00:24:10,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Would any central pool, movie, company, other people, would any of that would be able to make it worthwhile, if you think that you might die next second?

323
00:24:11,390 --> 00:24:11,570
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

324
00:24:11,770 --> 00:24:16,993
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you possibly accept further down the line things that depend upon your life, when your life is up?

325
00:24:18,698 --> 00:24:30,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why that is, like, if you maintain that kind of perspective, that kind of recollectedness, sati of death, you will abandon centrality, the very least.

326
00:24:34,142 --> 00:24:42,084
[SPEAKER_02]: So, let's say, engaging with a film, watching a film, it implies... Well, think of it like this.

327
00:24:42,104 --> 00:24:42,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Think of it practically.

328
00:24:43,625 --> 00:24:48,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't think you're going to die, and you want to watch a film, and you enjoy it.

329
00:24:48,606 --> 00:24:51,387
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a funny, good movie, but... Now...

330
00:24:52,175 --> 00:24:55,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Just before the movie, somebody tells you, I got your doctor's results.

331
00:24:56,016 --> 00:24:56,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

332
00:24:56,356 --> 00:24:58,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have 24 hours, maybe, at most.

333
00:24:58,517 --> 00:24:58,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

334
00:25:00,178 --> 00:25:02,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you go and watch the same movie and spend three hours watching it?

335
00:25:02,958 --> 00:25:04,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even just, you got six months to do it.

336
00:25:04,979 --> 00:25:06,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure, sure.

337
00:25:06,460 --> 00:25:07,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Even six months.

338
00:25:11,482 --> 00:25:19,025
[SPEAKER_01]: When somebody reorders the importance of the context for you, would you then engage with the movie?

339
00:25:19,306 --> 00:25:21,286
[SPEAKER_01]: You would have no desire for it either.

340
00:25:21,967 --> 00:25:25,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the necessary basis for your desire is lack of context.

341
00:25:25,809 --> 00:25:26,289
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

342
00:25:26,469 --> 00:25:29,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You think you can own the eyes.

343
00:25:29,370 --> 00:25:30,611
[SPEAKER_01]: You think you own the eyes.

344
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:32,940
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the owner of the eyes.

345
00:25:33,421 --> 00:25:34,601
[SPEAKER_01]: You took that for granted.

346
00:25:34,961 --> 00:25:38,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, now, let me find things that will engage my eyes presently.

347
00:25:39,923 --> 00:25:43,944
[SPEAKER_01]: But somebody comes and tells you you were going to lose... you're going to lose everything, not just your eyes.

348
00:25:44,765 --> 00:25:48,026
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you possibly then care for random sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches?

349
00:25:53,428 --> 00:25:59,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to wait for that to happen, because if you haven't practiced beforehand, it would be too late for you to make effort.

350
00:26:01,039 --> 00:26:06,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to start basically regaining, reclarifying, recollecting this perspective that is there.

351
00:26:06,224 --> 00:26:09,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You are truly like... the movie can be cancelled or not.

352
00:26:10,189 --> 00:26:12,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But one thing for sure, you cannot avoid dying.

353
00:26:12,671 --> 00:26:18,938
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's the thing, your enjoyment of the film requires a kind of...

354
00:26:20,097 --> 00:26:22,238
[SPEAKER_02]: What's happening next?

355
00:26:22,338 --> 00:26:22,899
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always...

356
00:26:22,919 --> 00:26:24,259
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires life to be there.

357
00:26:24,299 --> 00:26:24,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

358
00:26:24,800 --> 00:26:26,701
[SPEAKER_02]: It requires a kind of...

359
00:26:26,721 --> 00:26:30,222
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm going to find out what's... Well, that's what it means.

360
00:26:30,242 --> 00:26:31,963
[SPEAKER_01]: The sights need eyes.

361
00:26:32,644 --> 00:26:36,806
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there is no persistence of functioning eyes, sights are meaningless.

362
00:26:38,947 --> 00:26:40,208
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where life is.

363
00:26:40,608 --> 00:26:42,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Persistence of senses.

364
00:26:42,209 --> 00:26:43,109
[SPEAKER_01]: They're living senses.

365
00:26:43,610 --> 00:26:44,110
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm alive.

366
00:26:44,810 --> 00:26:46,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Senses are mine, thus this life is mine.

367
00:26:46,571 --> 00:26:47,412
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm in control of it.

368
00:26:49,237 --> 00:26:52,478
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you need to do is stop ignoring the fact that you already know.

369
00:26:53,658 --> 00:26:59,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's why, like, people, if you say something, oh, be careful, like, oh, no, no, don't say that, don't say that, like, phew!

370
00:26:59,820 --> 00:27:03,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, now you're gonna, you know, bad luck, because you said that you might go blind or something.

371
00:27:03,820 --> 00:27:04,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

372
00:27:04,521 --> 00:27:05,641
[SPEAKER_01]: No, but you might go blind.

373
00:27:06,941 --> 00:27:07,721
[SPEAKER_01]: You might get sick.

374
00:27:08,342 --> 00:27:12,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but you must not say it, because it makes me afraid and fills me with anxiety.

375
00:27:12,903 --> 00:27:15,523
[SPEAKER_01]: How could that be possible if you're not truly liable to it?

376
00:27:15,563 --> 00:27:16,003
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it really

377
00:27:18,940 --> 00:27:21,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear and anxiety are the reminders of that which you are liable to.

378
00:27:22,444 --> 00:27:32,994
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when I say, oh, please don't make me scared about, you know, that I'll die or go blind and lose my senses, means just don't remind me of what I already know, what I am subject to.

379
00:27:35,136 --> 00:27:37,259
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the that's the core of the Buddhist practice.

380
00:27:37,299 --> 00:27:40,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop ignoring that, by any means necessary.

381
00:27:42,823 --> 00:27:47,314
[SPEAKER_01]: The divine messengers, again, it's not like some poetic inspiration of how everybody will die.

382
00:27:47,354 --> 00:27:51,664
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's recognizing that it doesn't matter what you do, to what extent, for how long.

383
00:27:52,397 --> 00:27:53,057
[SPEAKER_01]: How many times?

384
00:27:53,078 --> 00:27:53,798
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

385
00:27:54,238 --> 00:27:54,999
[SPEAKER_01]: You are live.

386
00:27:55,079 --> 00:28:00,563
[SPEAKER_01]: You are, at any time, subject to death, subject to change, subject to loss.

387
00:28:00,863 --> 00:28:05,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, at any time, you're not in control of your own basis, of your own experience.

388
00:28:05,266 --> 00:28:06,427
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

389
00:28:06,447 --> 00:28:11,691
[SPEAKER_00]: So, firstly, recognize... that was the first step, recognizing that you're subject to birth, subject to sickness.

390
00:28:11,891 --> 00:28:16,274
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the first step, and that's the step within which every other step is contained.

391
00:28:16,334 --> 00:28:18,436
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, there was that sutra on the search.

392
00:28:18,916 --> 00:28:20,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I, who is subject to death,

393
00:28:36,815 --> 00:28:40,356
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm searching for something that is also subject to death.

394
00:28:40,436 --> 00:28:44,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I've got all this death, subject to deathness, with me.

395
00:28:44,578 --> 00:28:47,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now I must go and search for other things that are also death.

396
00:28:48,159 --> 00:28:49,119
[SPEAKER_00]: I've already fulfilled death.

397
00:28:51,220 --> 00:28:55,521
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're saying, like, you recognize that you are subject to loss.

398
00:28:56,462 --> 00:28:57,702
[SPEAKER_00]: You're subject to death, actually.

399
00:28:57,722 --> 00:29:02,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you don't really... Like, people, what you just described, that's not understanding you're subject to death.

400
00:29:04,178 --> 00:29:05,839
[SPEAKER_01]: People might say, yeah, I'm subject to death.

401
00:29:06,299 --> 00:29:07,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you see what you're going to do next.

402
00:29:08,381 --> 00:29:17,626
[SPEAKER_01]: If your next move is within something that's also subject to death, it means you're using that to basically distract yourself from the fact that you're subject to death.

403
00:29:18,487 --> 00:29:28,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not really staying on the level of, I am subject to death, because if you are, you would genuinely lose the desire for any further engagement down the line of death.

404
00:29:29,213 --> 00:29:30,334
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be inconceivable.

405
00:29:30,774 --> 00:29:31,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, am I...

406
00:29:32,715 --> 00:29:35,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I fully turned away from these things that are further within death?

407
00:29:35,918 --> 00:29:36,058
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

408
00:29:36,078 --> 00:29:39,380
[SPEAKER_01]: It means I haven't fully understood what being subject to death means.

409
00:29:39,560 --> 00:29:39,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry.

410
00:29:41,482 --> 00:29:49,207
[SPEAKER_00]: But as the Buddha was saying, you know... That's what the sense of urgency... Yeah, why am I... why must I go and seek that which is also subject to death?

411
00:29:49,388 --> 00:29:55,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Also subject to... And that becomes apparent when you truly feel that sense of urgency that you're subject to death.

412
00:29:55,592 --> 00:29:56,973
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, well, I feel this.

413
00:29:57,654 --> 00:29:58,494
[SPEAKER_01]: It's way too...

414
00:30:00,495 --> 00:30:03,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Threatening, too unpleasant, too serious to ignore.

415
00:30:04,198 --> 00:30:08,902
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can then I possibly go in search for other things that are within that depth?

416
00:30:11,184 --> 00:30:13,686
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not on the level of just reason it.

417
00:30:13,946 --> 00:30:14,927
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just reason it.

418
00:30:15,647 --> 00:30:23,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, people can reason it, which means it's going to be rooted in you trying to avoid it, or you covering up the perspective through your reasoning of it.

419
00:30:24,775 --> 00:30:29,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, practically speaking, it would be impossible to engage with sense objects,

420
00:30:30,495 --> 00:30:34,559
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're within the context of death, it's impossible.

421
00:30:34,879 --> 00:30:37,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you reverse that and have a look.

422
00:30:38,462 --> 00:30:39,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I engaging or not?

423
00:30:39,623 --> 00:30:40,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

424
00:30:40,103 --> 00:30:45,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, I cannot then at the same time think that I am established upon the the context of death.

425
00:30:45,428 --> 00:30:48,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I am engaging, I am still wanting these things.

426
00:30:50,352 --> 00:30:58,259
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, literally, so, if you have 24 hours or even less left to live,

427
00:31:00,843 --> 00:31:07,406
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is possible way out from that perilous situation that awaits for you.

428
00:31:08,967 --> 00:31:09,647
[SPEAKER_01]: What would you choose?

429
00:31:10,747 --> 00:31:11,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Finding the way out of death?

430
00:31:12,448 --> 00:31:16,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, oh, let me go and watch this movie and, like, make the most of my time that's left?

431
00:31:18,191 --> 00:31:20,312
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, it's, it's, the choice is automatic.

432
00:31:21,012 --> 00:31:25,594
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Guru would always say, for whoever, and literally, it doesn't matter if it's a patujan or an arahant,

433
00:31:26,291 --> 00:31:34,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you do something, whenever you want something, you do it because it's deep down inside, it is rooted in your notion of my own well-being.

434
00:31:34,738 --> 00:31:35,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

435
00:31:35,078 --> 00:31:37,380
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... you engage with desire because desire is unpleasant.

436
00:31:38,121 --> 00:31:40,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through engagement with it, you try to get rid of the pain.

437
00:31:41,982 --> 00:31:48,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Your engagement in pursuit of arahantship, because you understood that underneath it all, again, it's all... it's... it's... you're subjected to pain.

438
00:31:49,287 --> 00:31:52,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your own well-being is basically overcoming pain.

439
00:31:52,490 --> 00:31:56,332
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... by what means you do it, it's going to make you bound to pain or free from pain.

440
00:31:57,913 --> 00:32:03,377
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when it comes to such a clear-cut choice, death waits for you, possible escape or ignoring it.

441
00:32:04,058 --> 00:32:04,858
[SPEAKER_01]: The choice is automatic.

442
00:32:06,860 --> 00:32:11,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only reason you've been ignoring it before is because that was the closest to your means of escape.

443
00:32:22,227 --> 00:32:26,489
[SPEAKER_00]: That's quite a thought to have, if you weren't told before that.

444
00:32:27,070 --> 00:32:36,516
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm subject to death, but then the Buddha said, the thought process is, why don't I seek that which is deathless, which is not subject to death?

445
00:32:36,536 --> 00:32:38,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Why don't I seek the way out of death?

446
00:32:39,398 --> 00:32:40,859
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, who has that thought?

447
00:32:41,722 --> 00:32:44,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the person that has developed the context of death would.

448
00:32:46,045 --> 00:32:49,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think, back in the day, people were much less dishonest with themselves.

449
00:32:49,868 --> 00:32:52,290
[SPEAKER_01]: So, more of them would have had that, upon hearing the Buddha.

450
00:32:52,730 --> 00:32:59,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you need to unclutter your life seriously, mentally, in order just to gain the perspective of what you are subjected to, either way.

451
00:33:00,256 --> 00:33:02,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you get a sense of the escape from it.

452
00:33:03,279 --> 00:33:07,542
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you properly understand that I'm subject to death,

453
00:33:08,667 --> 00:33:11,289
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't be searching for...

454
00:33:11,689 --> 00:33:12,589
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be inconceivable.

455
00:33:12,609 --> 00:33:15,471
[SPEAKER_02]: ...something that is also subject to that.

456
00:33:15,611 --> 00:33:16,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it would be inconceivable.

457
00:33:17,092 --> 00:33:27,098
[SPEAKER_01]: And you even know, you can get a hint of that, you know, like when you get sick because you were careless, you were eating, you know, crap food or whatever else, and then there's a bit of a scare,

458
00:33:28,300 --> 00:33:33,983
[SPEAKER_01]: You're ill now, you've been ill for a week, you couldn't get out of the bed, food poisoning, and you're like, oh, I'll never do that again.

459
00:33:34,003 --> 00:33:38,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now, you know, the stakes are high, you feel it, the anxiety is there, a sense of urgency.

460
00:33:38,825 --> 00:33:43,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you recover, and completely, that context of that urgency of, I'll be careful next, is gone.

461
00:33:44,287 --> 00:33:45,448
[SPEAKER_01]: And there you go, you're doing it again.

462
00:33:46,708 --> 00:33:48,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is so fickle.

463
00:33:49,769 --> 00:33:52,411
[SPEAKER_01]: And the mind is fickle because you keep it so.

464
00:33:53,011 --> 00:33:55,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you, you, you basically, eh...

465
00:33:57,717 --> 00:33:59,638
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not rooting it in.

466
00:33:59,698 --> 00:34:00,578
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not training it.

467
00:34:00,738 --> 00:34:01,759
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not training against it.

468
00:34:01,779 --> 00:34:05,220
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just giving in to it, and that's... it's going to bring to its increase.

469
00:34:06,080 --> 00:34:07,201
[SPEAKER_01]: You're cultivating weakness.

470
00:34:11,623 --> 00:34:16,544
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's all like... like... it's... it's...

471
00:34:16,605 --> 00:34:22,767
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter if you're an absolute beginner, or if you've been practicing for, whatever, decades.

472
00:34:23,873 --> 00:34:27,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Reading the suttas and so on, that is the context that remains unchanged.

473
00:34:28,996 --> 00:34:34,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And that you should remain clarifying, and dwelling in, and maintaining, or protecting it, if you've developed it properly.

474
00:34:35,779 --> 00:34:36,920
[SPEAKER_01]: What you're subjected to it, actually.

475
00:34:37,560 --> 00:34:40,321
[SPEAKER_01]: And... because death means impossible to own.

476
00:34:40,741 --> 00:34:42,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Death means lack of ownership, utterly.

477
00:34:43,743 --> 00:34:47,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Death means things that I own, are actually rooted in that which I can't own.

478
00:34:49,162 --> 00:34:52,504
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you're just just starting about Buddhism, that's your practice.

479
00:34:52,664 --> 00:34:56,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep the precepts that will basically train you in strength of restraint.

480
00:34:57,746 --> 00:35:07,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't, and then you start thinking about this death and stuff, well, you might be flying left and right, in terms of things you decide to do, or outrun.

481
00:35:08,952 --> 00:35:14,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you've said no to that type of engagement with sense objects.

482
00:35:15,085 --> 00:35:17,086
[SPEAKER_01]: And you start clarifying what you are subjected to.

483
00:35:17,266 --> 00:35:27,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you will be able to withstand anxiety, sense of urgency, all these things, without, like, panicking and giving in to ridiculous actions of even madness, and so on.

484
00:35:29,469 --> 00:35:30,709
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's not optional.

485
00:35:31,729 --> 00:35:36,491
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no other way than the way of the divine messengers that the Buddha described.

486
00:35:36,751 --> 00:35:41,832
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just whether you're going to equip yourself to be able to withstand their way, or not.

487
00:35:41,972 --> 00:35:44,513
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, sometimes someone's progress is painful,

488
00:35:45,342 --> 00:35:46,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Someone else's isn't.

489
00:35:46,603 --> 00:35:47,825
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same way.

490
00:35:48,345 --> 00:35:49,526
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be the same way.

491
00:35:50,407 --> 00:36:00,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're going to suffer on account of it or not, is whether you can take it or not.

492
00:36:00,497 --> 00:36:05,843
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if somebody is not interested in the complete freedom from suffering and arahantship,

493
00:36:06,487 --> 00:36:09,331
[SPEAKER_01]: A layman, but still values the Buddha's teaching, that's your practice.

494
00:36:09,692 --> 00:36:18,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep the precepts and whatever you engage from the moment you wake up, the moment you go to sleep, try and have it against the backdrop of, I am subject to death.

495
00:36:19,087 --> 00:36:20,228
[SPEAKER_01]: I might not live the next day.

496
00:36:21,863 --> 00:36:24,844
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, everything will be... well, it will even out.

497
00:36:25,725 --> 00:36:33,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Even as a lay person who still engages with family, centrality, duties, distraction, it will significantly even out.

498
00:36:33,989 --> 00:36:36,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Your desires, your passions will even out.

499
00:36:37,290 --> 00:36:39,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you do... you refuse to, like, give in completely.

500
00:36:39,992 --> 00:36:49,596
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep at least one percent of your decision to engage, have it against the fundamental context that, fundamentally, things will be taken away.

501
00:36:50,157 --> 00:36:50,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot be mined.

502
00:36:52,060 --> 00:36:55,642
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically beginning of wisdom, even for a householder.

503
00:36:58,103 --> 00:36:59,824
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could see how different that is.

504
00:37:00,745 --> 00:37:15,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, actively thinking the thoughts of the context that you're subjected to, as opposed to having your practice of just mindlessly watching your nostrils for an hour a day, and then go about carelessly giving in to desire and everything else, because you've done your duty.

505
00:37:17,093 --> 00:37:18,574
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you don't have to watch your nostrils at all.

506
00:37:21,307 --> 00:37:22,467
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to meditate at all.

507
00:37:23,888 --> 00:37:26,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this will be the meditation, basically.

508
00:37:26,668 --> 00:37:30,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of, this is when I do it, it's done all the time.

509
00:37:35,050 --> 00:37:37,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, putting things in the right perspective.

510
00:37:37,811 --> 00:37:40,332
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you put something in perspective, if you have no perspective?

511
00:37:41,952 --> 00:37:45,713
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you gain perspective, unless you start investigating where the perspective is?

512
00:37:50,180 --> 00:37:55,264
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, like, if you're a monk and committed a whole life to this practice, same.

513
00:37:56,024 --> 00:37:59,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you're about to do, whatever you're doing against the book, you might not live through this.

514
00:38:01,388 --> 00:38:02,349
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a valid thought.

515
00:38:02,609 --> 00:38:03,610
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a valid context.

516
00:38:05,551 --> 00:38:11,736
[SPEAKER_02]: So, could you say then, say a lay person's meditation could be just

517
00:38:15,313 --> 00:38:17,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Rather than staring at the nostrils for half an hour...

518
00:38:17,874 --> 00:38:18,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Reflection.

519
00:38:18,414 --> 00:38:21,635
[SPEAKER_01]: ...thinking about clarifying the death.

520
00:38:21,755 --> 00:38:23,776
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a samādhi without sati.

521
00:38:24,776 --> 00:38:27,517
[SPEAKER_01]: And sati, as we said, it's recollecting the right context.

522
00:38:28,117 --> 00:38:29,077
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's your samādhi.

523
00:38:29,137 --> 00:38:37,340
[SPEAKER_01]: When that context, it becomes less changeable or harder to lose, you can say you have samādhi.

524
00:38:40,839 --> 00:38:41,919
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, absolutely.

525
00:38:42,220 --> 00:38:47,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Your practice of samādhi and concentration should be practice of recollecting the right context.

526
00:38:48,122 --> 00:38:48,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is?

527
00:38:48,362 --> 00:38:55,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Context of death, context of aging, context of sickness, context that it's impossible to own the eye organ, it's impossible to own the nose organ, and so on.

528
00:38:57,205 --> 00:39:04,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if I cannot own these eyes, then how can any sight sound, any sights that come through it, or any sounds, how can that possibly be mine?

529
00:39:05,028 --> 00:39:08,529
[SPEAKER_01]: When, like, sights are inconceivable without the necessary basis of eyes.

530
00:39:09,170 --> 00:39:09,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Functioning eyes.

531
00:39:13,034 --> 00:39:21,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about that, feel it, sense of urgency, allow it to endure, and restraint will become effortless.

532
00:39:22,280 --> 00:39:24,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, certainly, it will require less effort.

533
00:39:25,903 --> 00:39:26,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

534
00:39:26,103 --> 00:39:33,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you realize, by not engaging, I'm not exposing myself to risk of death, of losing, of being.

535
00:39:33,108 --> 00:39:36,190
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha compared going down the path of sensuality.

536
00:39:36,210 --> 00:39:39,192
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like climbing up the tree to eat the mangoes at the top.

537
00:39:40,408 --> 00:39:43,971
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, exposing yourself to the risk of falling down, somebody chopping the tree.

538
00:39:44,011 --> 00:39:45,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Millions of reasons can happen to that tree.

539
00:39:46,253 --> 00:39:49,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you come down, you will suffer on account of that.

540
00:39:49,836 --> 00:39:52,258
[SPEAKER_01]: If you come down, doesn't matter if somebody cuts the trees.

541
00:39:52,798 --> 00:39:54,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if somebody burned the whole cinema down.

542
00:39:55,660 --> 00:39:57,402
[SPEAKER_01]: I lost desire in watching movies there.

543
00:39:58,042 --> 00:40:01,425
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not affect me.

544
00:40:05,688 --> 00:40:06,789
[SPEAKER_00]: You have that backdrop.

545
00:40:07,150 --> 00:40:07,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

546
00:40:08,126 --> 00:40:16,454
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness, as in recollectedness of the backdrop, the right backdrop, the right context, and samādhi is maintaining it and protecting it.

547
00:40:17,576 --> 00:40:19,417
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would result in fully understanding it.

548
00:40:19,578 --> 00:40:26,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness, that was obviously the result of your of your restraint, not engaging by default.

549
00:40:27,465 --> 00:40:29,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Default have a watch a movie, I watch a movie.

550
00:40:30,468 --> 00:40:31,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense restraint.

551
00:40:31,249 --> 00:40:31,830
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sīla,

552
00:40:33,851 --> 00:40:36,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that mindfulness, bring samādhi.

553
00:40:36,532 --> 00:40:38,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Just fully establish this.

554
00:40:38,212 --> 00:40:41,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the context remains enduring, despite what's happening around you.

555
00:40:41,953 --> 00:40:43,593
[SPEAKER_01]: The context doesn't need to change.

556
00:40:43,613 --> 00:40:44,313
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

557
00:40:45,053 --> 00:40:46,134
[SPEAKER_01]: You only need to protect it.

558
00:40:46,294 --> 00:40:47,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, you protect it from yourself.

559
00:40:48,894 --> 00:40:52,715
[SPEAKER_01]: You protect the context from you abandoning the context.

560
00:40:54,336 --> 00:41:00,917
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you stop doing things that would interfere with the context, that's how you protect it.

561
00:41:02,002 --> 00:41:10,349
[SPEAKER_01]: And the natural result of that is fully understanding of that context and fully understanding of things what you used to do and how you used to do them in order to basically avoid facing the context.

562
00:41:10,829 --> 00:41:19,176
[SPEAKER_00]: When that context is always there, steady, composed, samādhi, then you can't dictate your actions, your decisions in the future.

563
00:41:19,657 --> 00:41:20,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you don't need to do an action.

564
00:41:20,838 --> 00:41:22,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, and now I will not go and engage with it.

565
00:41:23,200 --> 00:41:25,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-engagement will be automatic.

566
00:41:25,802 --> 00:41:28,644
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sīla brings samādhi, brings pañña.

567
00:41:28,704 --> 00:41:29,445
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how it works.

568
00:41:33,552 --> 00:41:36,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the... And either way, sati is there.

569
00:41:36,495 --> 00:41:38,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Recollectedness of the context is there.

570
00:41:38,357 --> 00:41:41,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Necessary for sīla, necessary for samādhi, necessary for pañña.

571
00:41:42,202 --> 00:41:48,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever happens in life, something comes up, but there's a building possibility for a building project.

572
00:41:50,315 --> 00:41:53,257
[SPEAKER_00]: If you've got the backdrop of, I'm going to die...

573
00:41:53,277 --> 00:41:55,718
[SPEAKER_01]: I might not live for tomorrow, let alone a ten-year project.

574
00:41:55,738 --> 00:41:58,780
[SPEAKER_01]: You know how far you're going to be engaged in that project.

575
00:41:58,800 --> 00:41:59,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

576
00:41:59,520 --> 00:42:04,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can engage, and literally, like, on a daily basis, so to speak.

577
00:42:04,683 --> 00:42:08,125
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to make that decision that every day I will re-evaluate and re-engage with it.

578
00:42:08,165 --> 00:42:08,345
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

579
00:42:08,806 --> 00:42:12,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Just make a decision to not lose the context that you might not live through.

580
00:42:13,328 --> 00:42:16,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, whenever you end up being engaged, it's going to be at the arm's length.

581
00:42:16,371 --> 00:42:19,053
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be doing that which is actually practically important.

582
00:42:19,313 --> 00:42:19,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

583
00:42:19,814 --> 00:42:24,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather than passionately... Passionate, blind, sort of self-fueling ambition.

584
00:42:25,139 --> 00:42:25,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

585
00:42:25,359 --> 00:42:26,560
[SPEAKER_01]: That will become inconceivable.

586
00:42:28,618 --> 00:42:43,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, there's a sutta that says, develop... the Buddha says, develop composure, develop samādhi, bhikkhus, because a bhikkhu with samādhi developed, sees things as they really are.

587
00:42:44,728 --> 00:42:46,109
[SPEAKER_02]: What does he see as they really are?

588
00:42:46,989 --> 00:42:51,032
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't remember if it's the aggregates or the senses, but... Sure.

589
00:42:51,072 --> 00:42:51,352
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

590
00:42:53,831 --> 00:42:55,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they are already like that.

591
00:42:55,532 --> 00:43:00,454
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but you're now... the context is now firmly established.

592
00:43:00,494 --> 00:43:02,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Not having the right context means having the wrong context.

593
00:43:03,055 --> 00:43:03,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

594
00:43:03,775 --> 00:43:04,756
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing in between.

595
00:43:04,896 --> 00:43:05,196
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

596
00:43:05,556 --> 00:43:08,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means the wrong context will be obscuring how things are.

597
00:43:08,937 --> 00:43:10,658
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what the Māra says.

598
00:43:12,259 --> 00:43:13,719
[SPEAKER_01]: But, like, no, this is yours.

599
00:43:13,959 --> 00:43:14,580
[SPEAKER_01]: This is mine.

600
00:43:14,820 --> 00:43:15,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

601
00:43:15,260 --> 00:43:16,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And the mind is there.

602
00:43:16,460 --> 00:43:17,301
[SPEAKER_01]: You're affected by it.

603
00:43:17,581 --> 00:43:18,961
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the Māra controls you.

604
00:43:20,442 --> 00:43:22,823
[SPEAKER_01]: And Māra himself cannot see anything other than ownership.

605
00:43:26,364 --> 00:43:27,084
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why...

606
00:43:27,124 --> 00:43:33,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through Mara's ownership, through Mara's domain, you understand the nature of that domain, and that's how you stop owning it.

607
00:43:33,467 --> 00:43:35,448
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha says, even my path you will not see.

608
00:43:35,888 --> 00:43:36,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

609
00:43:36,949 --> 00:43:37,869
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean how I used to be.

610
00:43:37,929 --> 00:43:38,089
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

611
00:43:38,229 --> 00:43:38,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

612
00:43:38,570 --> 00:43:40,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Past is understood against the same backdrop.

613
00:43:40,730 --> 00:43:41,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Future is understood.

614
00:43:42,031 --> 00:43:45,853
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there is no more wandering about in saṃsara.

615
00:43:47,594 --> 00:43:48,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you understood the aggregates.

616
00:43:48,814 --> 00:43:51,295
[SPEAKER_01]: You understood the framework that is impossible to own.

617
00:43:52,216 --> 00:43:52,576
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

618
00:43:59,003 --> 00:44:00,404
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what we said before.

619
00:44:00,784 --> 00:44:04,426
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't lose what you recollected, accidentally.

620
00:44:05,606 --> 00:44:10,309
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you recollected the context of death correctly, you don't need to say, okay, I'm gonna die, I'm gonna die.

621
00:44:10,349 --> 00:44:10,489
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

622
00:44:10,649 --> 00:44:11,569
[SPEAKER_01]: You recollected it's there.

623
00:44:11,930 --> 00:44:16,552
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is keep a corner of your eye on what you're about to do next.

624
00:44:16,572 --> 00:44:18,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Not what you're doing, but what you're planning to do.

625
00:44:19,353 --> 00:44:20,494
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always planning something to do.

626
00:44:20,914 --> 00:44:21,714
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always implicit.

627
00:44:21,774 --> 00:44:24,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Possibility of extending what I'm doing now, whatever, has to be there.

628
00:44:25,936 --> 00:44:26,856
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you keep an eye on.

629
00:44:26,956 --> 00:44:28,737
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you protect that that doorway.

630
00:44:29,617 --> 00:44:31,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so I am subject to death.

631
00:44:31,117 --> 00:44:31,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what am I doing?

632
00:44:31,677 --> 00:44:32,398
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sitting here.

633
00:44:33,718 --> 00:44:37,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, now I'm giving in to such and such thoughts of sensual nature or nature of ill will.

634
00:44:38,559 --> 00:44:44,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you can only do that by choosing to do it, which means then choosing to turn away from the context of death.

635
00:44:46,001 --> 00:44:49,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't make the choice to turn away from it, that's it.

636
00:44:49,482 --> 00:44:52,082
[SPEAKER_01]: The context of you being subjected to death is still there.

637
00:44:53,503 --> 00:44:54,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you protect it.

638
00:44:55,113 --> 00:44:58,813
[SPEAKER_01]: By not intentionally throwing it away.

639
00:45:00,162 --> 00:45:03,064
[SPEAKER_01]: And the clearer that becomes, the easier to protect it becomes.

640
00:45:03,084 --> 00:45:08,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you think that all these things are happening random to me, and these random things can take away my mindfulness.

641
00:45:08,627 --> 00:45:08,787
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

642
00:45:09,448 --> 00:45:15,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever mindfulness is taken away, means there has been setting in of desire, aversion or distraction.

643
00:45:15,791 --> 00:45:18,193
[SPEAKER_01]: And those things cannot not be intentional.

644
00:45:18,913 --> 00:45:19,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry.

645
00:45:19,513 --> 00:45:20,634
[SPEAKER_01]: They're always intentional.

646
00:45:20,794 --> 00:45:23,756
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot accidentally engage with sensual desire.

647
00:45:24,216 --> 00:45:26,778
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the level of the thought that you've said yes to.

648
00:45:28,337 --> 00:45:32,882
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot accidentally perform an act of ill-will, or accidentally distract yourself.

649
00:45:33,002 --> 00:45:35,225
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always rooted in you choosing to do so.

650
00:45:36,426 --> 00:45:40,331
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop choosing to do so, you cannot possibly obscure the context then.

